/// \defgroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers WW3D2 render-object base, hierarchy pivots, light environment and vertex formats
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief The base render object's copy, bounds cache, quick ray test,
///        sub-object lookup, bone attachment and scene notifications; the
///        hierarchy tree's bone lookups and base-pose pivot evaluation; the
///        pivot record's copy operations; the light environment's
///        constructor; and the flexible-vertex-format name and size helpers.
///
/// All of this is WW3D2, the Westwood 3D library under
/// Code/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WW3D2/ (\ref sub_w3d_rendering describes the
/// library and the device layer above it). The names are Westwood's, from
/// Zero Hour's rendobj, htree, pivot, lightenvironment and dx8fvf units, and
/// each body here is reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code. To see where
/// the project keeps a body, search reverse/functions.csv for the function's
/// name with rg.
///
/// - Render objects. RenderObjClass is the base of everything the renderer
///   draws. RenderObjClass::operator= copies the base state,
///   RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes refreshes the world-space
///   bounds that culling and picking use, RenderObjClass::Intersect_Sphere_Quick
///   is the cheap ray test, RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name and
///   RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone find and attach sub-objects, and
///   RenderObjClass::Notify_Added and RenderObjClass::Notify_Removed track the
///   scene an object is in.
/// - Skeletons. HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name and HTreeClass::Get_Parent_Index read
///   the bone table, and HTreeClass::Simple_Evaluate_Pivot computes one bone's
///   world transform in the rest pose. PivotClass is one bone;
///   PivotClass::PivotClass and PivotClass::operator= copy it.
/// - Lighting. LightEnvironmentClass collects the few lights that affect one
///   object; LightEnvironmentClass::LightEnvironmentClass sets it up empty.
/// - Vertex formats. FVFInfoClass::Get_FVF_Name names a Direct3D flexible
///   vertex format for debugging, and Get_FVF_Vertex_Size returns a format's
///   vertex size in bytes.
///
/// Neighbouring pages: \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers holds the HTreeClass
/// class page with the BFME 2 pivot layout, HTreeClass::Init_Default and
/// SegLineRendererClass::operator=; \ref grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// holds the SegLineRendererClass class page,
/// RenderObjClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box and LightClass.
/// HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Index, the name-to-index search that
/// RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone relies on, lives in the same unit
/// as HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name; it compares names case-insensitively and
/// returns 0, the root, for a name it does not find.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// Established by comparing the BFME 2 bodies with Zero Hour's source:
/// - RenderObjClass::operator= copies the source's transform and its
///   identity flag. Zero Hour's marks the destination's transform as
///   not-identity and copies the native screen size instead.
/// - RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name skips sub-objects that have no
///   name. Zero Hour's passes whatever name it gets to the string functions.
/// - RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone takes a third argument, an
///   optional offset, and passes it on.
/// - HTreeClass::Simple_Evaluate_Pivot works on BFME 2's pivots, which store
///   a rotation quaternion and a translation instead of a matrix, and checks
///   its output pointer before it writes through it.
/// - LightEnvironmentClass's constructor clears every light slot, and the
///   class has one extra leading field.
///
/// RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes,
/// RenderObjClass::Intersect_Sphere_Quick, the two scene notifications,
/// HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name, HTreeClass::Get_Parent_Index and
/// FVFInfoClass::Get_FVF_Name do what Zero Hour's do.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name and HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Index
///   compare names case-insensitively. Get_Sub_Object_By_Name also accepts
///   the part of a sub-object's name after its first period. (Zero Hour
///   only: W3DModelDraw resolves its ShowSubObject and HideSubObject entries
///   through this function, so they can omit the model-name prefix.) No
///   BFME 2 data path to this function is established.
/// - RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone does not fail on a bone name the
///   skeleton lacks: it attaches to bone 0, the root, because the name search
///   returns 0. Which game data reaches this function in BFME 2 is not
///   established.
/// - An object keeps at most four lights in its light slots, the computed
///   fill light included when it is added (LightEnvironmentClass).
///   LightEnvironmentClass::Add_Light keeps the slots sorted by strength and
///   adds each accepted light's ambient component to the object's ambient
///   colour before it decides whether the light gets a slot, so weaker
///   lights beyond the four strongest still change the ambient colour; only
///   their per-light part (direction and diffuse colour) is dropped. A light
///   whose diffuse colour is near black is rejected before either step.
///   When LightEnvironmentClass::Add_Fill_Light runs with all four slots in
///   use, the fill light replaces the last and weakest light; a near-black
///   fill light takes no slot and only adds to the ambient colour.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Vertex layouts are Direct3D flexible-vertex-format codes, and BFME 2
///   asks D3DX for their sizes. The executable imports D3DXGetFVFVertexSize
///   from the D3DX 9 runtime (d3dx9_27.dll); see Get_FVF_Vertex_Size. A port
///   to another graphics API replaces the codes with its own vertex layout
///   descriptions and removes the D3DX dependency.
/// - Per-object lighting keeps at most four lights and an ambient colour
///   per object, in fixed arrays (LightEnvironmentClass). A per-pixel
///   renderer can light from the scene's lights directly, but code such as
///   DX8Wrapper::Set_Light_Environment and the mesh's copy of the
///   environment assume those arrays.
/// - Cached world-space bounds drive culling. The bounding sphere's radius
///   grows with the object's own scale factor but not with any scale held in
///   its transform (RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes); a port
///   that scales objects through the matrix must account for that.


/// \file WW3D2/dx8fvf.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Flexible-vertex-format helpers: FVFInfoClass's name formatter and
///        the vertex-size wrapper Get_FVF_Vertex_Size.
///
/// Ported from Zero Hour's dx8fvf.cpp. The unit also reconstructs BFME 2's
/// larger vertex-format descriptor, which docs/reconstruction/dx8fvf-descriptor.md
/// describes; see FVFInfoClass.


/// \class RenderObjClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief The base class of every W3D render object: meshes, hierarchical
///        models, lights, cameras, particles, lines and the rest of what a
///        scene draws.
///
/// A render object has a transform, cached world-space bounds, a set of
/// visibility and collision flags, and a place in the scene graph: the scene
/// it belongs to and, for a sub-object, the container it is attached to. Its
/// virtual interface covers rendering, sub-objects, bones, animation,
/// collision and intersection tests; many base versions do nothing or
/// report nothing, and derived classes override what applies to them. In BFME 2
/// the classes that build on it include MeshClass, HLodClass, LightClass,
/// CameraClass, ParticleEmitterClass, ParticleBufferClass,
/// SegmentedLineClass, StreakLineClass, Line3DClass, BoxRenderObjClass,
/// Null3DObjClass, CompositeRenderObjClass and the terrain's
/// BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass, whose assignment operators or scene
/// notifications call the base versions documented here.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - Render objects are reference counted (the class derives from
///   RefCountClass) and can sit in several lists at once (it also derives
///   from MultiListObjectClass). Functions that hand out a sub-object, such
///   as RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name, return it with a reference
///   the caller must release.
/// - A top-level object knows its scene but has no container; a sub-object
///   has both. RenderObjClass::Notify_Added and RenderObjClass::Notify_Removed
///   set and clear the scene. Copying an object, by the copy constructor or
///   RenderObjClass::operator=, never copies its scene, container or user
///   data.
///
/// \par Bounds cache
/// The world-space bounding sphere and box are computed on demand and kept
/// until the transform changes. BFME 2's Set_Transform and Set_Position
/// clear the cache's valid flag, as Zero Hour's do; Get_Bounding_Sphere and
/// Get_Bounding_Box call RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes
/// through the virtual table when the flag is clear, and that function sets
/// it again.
///
/// \par BFME 2 notes
/// Established from the BFME 2 bodies:
/// - When a top-level object in a scene moves, BFME 2's Set_Transform and
///   Set_Position call the scene's Register function with a registration
///   reason that Zero Hour's scene interface does not have; Zero Hour's only
///   invalidate the bounds. What the scene does with it is not established
///   here.
/// - The object is larger than Zero Hour's. Among the additions are two
///   per-object factors that the constructor sets to 1.0, read and written
///   through a pair of indexed virtual accessors, with a further virtual
///   returning their product. What they control is not established. The
///   copy constructor copies them from the source;
///   RenderObjClass::operator= does not (see there).
///
/// \see RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes,
///      RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name,
///      RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone, RenderObjClass::Notify_Added,
///      RenderObjClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box, \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn RenderObjClass &RenderObjClass::operator=(const RenderObjClass &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Copies the base render-object state that an assignment should
///        carry: visibility and collision settings and the transform.
///
/// Derived classes' assignment operators call it to copy the base part.
/// Its callers include the assignment operators of LightClass,
/// CameraClass, MeshClass, SegmentedLineClass, StreakLineClass,
/// Line3DClass, BoxRenderObjClass and Null3DObjClass.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Assigning an object to itself does nothing. Otherwise:
/// - the hidden, animation-hidden and force-visible states and the
///   collision type are copied through their virtual setters, so a derived
///   class that overrides a setter sees the change;
/// - the transform and the flag that records whether it is the identity are
///   copied directly;
/// - for each of BFME 2's two per-object factors (see the class page) the
///   function reads the value and stores it back through the virtual
///   accessors, but both calls are made on the destination object, so the
///   destination keeps its own factors. This is what retail does; the copy
///   constructor, by contrast, copies the factors from the source.
///
/// The scene, the container, the user data, the cached bounds and the
/// object scale are not copied. The cached bounds are not invalidated
/// either, so unless something else invalidates them, after an assignment
/// that changes the transform they still describe the old position.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's operator copies the native screen size and marks the
///       destination's transform as not-identity, but copies no transform.
///
/// \param that The object to copy from.
/// \return This object.
///
/// \see RenderObjClass, RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes


/// \fn void RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes(void) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Recomputes the object's world-space bounding sphere and box from
///        its object-space bounds and its transform, and marks them valid.
///
/// A protected virtual function, reached through the virtual table: the
/// bounding-volume getters (Get_Bounding_Sphere, Get_Bounding_Box) call it
/// when the cache is out of date, which happens after every change of
/// transform. The results are what view culling and the intersection tests,
/// such as RenderObjClass::Intersect_Sphere_Quick, work with. Zero Hour's
/// overrides include those of MeshClass, ParticleBufferClass,
/// ParticleEmitterClass and SphereRenderObjClass.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Asks the object for its object-space box and sphere through the virtual
///   Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box and Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Sphere.
/// - Moves the sphere's centre by the transform and multiplies its radius by
///   the object's scale factor. The transform's own rotation does not affect
///   a sphere, and any scale it carries is not applied to the radius.
/// - Transforms the box by the transform, which yields the axis-aligned box
///   that encloses the rotated original.
/// - Sets the cache's valid flag.
///
/// The cache members are mutable, which is why a const function can refresh
/// them. The body is Zero Hour's unchanged.
///
/// \see RenderObjClass, RenderObjClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box,
///      RenderObjClass::Intersect_Sphere_Quick


/// \fn bool RenderObjClass::Intersect_Sphere_Quick(IntersectionClass *Intersection, IntersectionResultClass *Final_Result)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief The cheap first test of a picking ray: does the line of the ray
///        pass through the object's world-space bounding sphere?
///
/// A virtual function. Zero Hour: RenderObjClass::Intersect runs it first
/// and casts the ray against the real geometry only when it passes. The ray
/// comes in an IntersectionClass, which Zero Hour can also build from a
/// screen position. In BFME 2 it is reached through the virtual table, and
/// no direct caller is identified.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Takes the bounding sphere from Get_Bounding_Sphere, which refreshes the
/// cache if needed (RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes). With v
/// the vector from the ray's origin to the sphere's centre and d the ray's
/// direction, it stores
/// - Alpha = v . d, the distance along the ray to the point nearest the
///   centre (d is assumed to be of unit length);
/// - Beta = r * r - (v . v - Alpha * Alpha), the squared radius less the
///   squared distance between the line and the centre;
///
/// and the result is whether Beta is non-negative, which it also stores in
/// the result's Intersects flag. The test is against the whole line: a
/// sphere behind the ray's origin or beyond its maximum distance still
/// passes. IntersectionClass::Intersect_Sphere uses Alpha and Beta to find
/// the actual hit point. BFME 2's body is the inlined form of Zero Hour's
/// IntersectionClass::Intersect_Sphere_Quick helper, with the same
/// arithmetic.
///
/// \param Intersection The ray: its origin and direction pointers are read.
/// \param Final_Result [out] Receives Alpha, Beta and the Intersects flag.
/// \return True when the line of the ray meets the bounding sphere.
///
/// \see RenderObjClass::Update_Cached_Bounding_Volumes, RenderObjClass


/// \fn RenderObjClass *RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name(const char *name, int *index) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Finds a sub-object by name, trying full names first and then the
///        part of each name after its first period.
///
/// A virtual function that works for any container, since it only uses the
/// virtual Get_Num_Sub_Objects, Get_Sub_Object and Get_Name. Zero Hour's
/// W3DModelDraw uses it to find the sub-objects that model-condition data
/// shows and hides. In BFME 2 it is reached through the virtual table, and
/// no direct caller is identified.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - First pass: compares each sub-object's full name with the requested
///   name, ignoring case (_strcmpi).
/// - Second pass: compares the part of each name after its first period,
///   or the whole name when it has none, so for a sub-object named
///   "MODEL.PART" a caller can pass just "PART". (Zero Hour: this lets
///   W3DModelDraw's ShowSubObject and HideSubObject entries omit the
///   model-name prefix.)
/// - Sub-objects without a name are skipped in both passes, which Zero
///   Hour's version does not do.
/// - Each sub-object fetched but not chosen is released. The one returned
///   keeps the reference Get_Sub_Object gave it.
///
/// The first match wins, so two parts with the same suffix under different
/// prefixes resolve to whichever comes first.
///
/// \param name  The name to look for. Must not be null.
/// \param index [out] When not null, receives the matching sub-object's
///              index. Left unchanged when nothing matches.
/// \return The sub-object, with a reference the caller must release, or
///         null when no sub-object matches.
///
/// \see RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone, RenderObjClass


/// \fn int RenderObjClass::Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone(RenderObjClass *subobj, const char *bname, const Vector3 *offset)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Attaches an object to the bone with the given name.
///
/// The by-name form of the virtual attachment call. It turns the name into a
/// bone index with the virtual Get_Bone_Index and calls the index form,
/// which the classes that have bones override; the base class's index form
/// attaches nothing and returns 0. In BFME 2 it is reached through the
/// virtual table, and no direct caller is identified.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Two virtual calls and nothing else: Get_Bone_Index(bname), then
/// Add_Sub_Object_To_Bone(subobj, index, offset). For an animated model,
/// Get_Bone_Index searches the skeleton (HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Index), which
/// answers 0, the root bone, for a name it does not know, so an unknown
/// name attaches to the root rather than failing. Zero Hour documents the
/// same behaviour.
///
/// \note The offset parameter is BFME 2's; Zero Hour's function has only
///       the object and the bone name. BFME 2's HLodClass index form stores
///       the offset with the attached model, or a zero offset when none is
///       given. How the offset is applied when the attachment is positioned
///       is not established here.
///
/// \param subobj The object to attach. HLodClass's index form takes its
///               own reference to it.
/// \param bname  The bone's name, matched case-insensitively.
/// \param offset An optional offset to attach with; may be null.
/// \return Whatever the index form returns; the base class returns 0.
///
/// \see RenderObjClass::Get_Sub_Object_By_Name, HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name,
///      RenderObjClass


/// \fn void RenderObjClass::Notify_Added(SceneClass *scene)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Records the scene the object has been added to.
///
/// Zero Hour: SceneClass::Add_Render_Object calls this virtual
/// (SimpleSceneClass does so before adding the object to its render list).
/// Some overrides register the object with the scene, for example for
/// per-frame updates or as a light; containers forward the call to their
/// sub-objects, so every object in a model learns its scene. Overrides call
/// this base version to store the pointer; in BFME 2 its callers include the
/// overrides of BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass, LightClass, HLodClass,
/// ParticleEmitterClass, ParticleBufferClass and CompositeRenderObjClass.
/// BFME 2's HLodClass also calls Notify_Added on an object it attaches to a
/// bone while it is already in a scene.
///
/// The stored scene is what Is_In_Scene and Peek_Scene report, and BFME 2's
/// Set_Transform and Set_Position use it to notify the scene when a
/// top-level object moves (see the class page).
///
/// \param scene The scene the object now belongs to.
///
/// \see RenderObjClass::Notify_Removed, RenderObjClass


/// \fn void RenderObjClass::Notify_Removed(SceneClass *scene)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Forgets the scene the object belonged to.
///
/// The counterpart of RenderObjClass::Notify_Added. Zero Hour:
/// SceneClass::Remove_Render_Object calls it, and containers pass it on to
/// their sub-objects. In BFME 2 the callers of this base version include
/// the overrides of LightClass, HLodClass, ParticleEmitterClass,
/// ParticleBufferClass and CompositeRenderObjClass.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Clears the scene pointer; the argument is not used.
///
/// \param scene The scene the object is leaving (ignored).
///
/// \see RenderObjClass::Notify_Added


/// \fn const char *HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name(int boneid) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Returns the name of the bone with the given index.
///
/// Animatable3DObjClass::Get_Bone_Name forwards to it when the model has a
/// skeleton, and answers "RootTransform" when it has none.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Returns the pivot's name buffer. The source asserts that the index is in
/// range, but the release build has no asserts, so the retail function does
/// no checking: an out-of-range index reads outside the pivot array.
/// The name is the one stored in the hierarchy chunk of the W3D file; Zero
/// Hour's W3D format gives names a fixed 16-byte field, and BFME 2's pivot
/// keeps a 16-character buffer.
///
/// \param boneid The bone's index, from 0 (the root) to one less than the
///               pivot count.
/// \return The bone's name. The string belongs to the tree.
///
/// \see HTreeClass::Get_Parent_Index, HTreeClass, PivotClass


/// \fn int HTreeClass::Get_Parent_Index(int bone_indx) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Returns the index of a bone's parent, or 0 for the root.
///
/// Lets code walk up the skeleton. Its identified caller is
/// doHideShowBoneSubObjs, a W3DModelDraw helper that hides or shows every
/// sub-object attached to a bone below a given bone (Zero Hour runs it after
/// hiding or showing a named sub-object). For each sub-object it climbs from
/// the sub-object's bone towards the root and stops when it reaches the
/// given bone or index 0.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads the pivot's parent pointer and returns that pivot's index; a pivot
/// without a parent gives 0. Only the root has no parent, and its own index
/// is also 0, so "the parent is the root" and "there is no parent" give the
/// same answer, which is what makes 0 a safe stopping point. As with
/// HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name, the range asserts are not in the release
/// build.
///
/// \param bone_indx The bone's index.
/// \return The parent's index, or 0 when the bone has no parent.
///
/// \see HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Name, HTreeClass


/// \fn bool HTreeClass::Simple_Evaluate_Pivot(int pivot_index, const Matrix3D &obj_tm, Matrix3D *end_tm) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Computes one bone's world transform in the skeleton's rest pose,
///        without updating the whole tree.
///
/// The cheap way to ask where a bone is when no animation is playing.
/// Animatable3DObjClass::Simple_Evaluate_Bone calls it, with the object's
/// transform, when the model has no animation or is set to its base pose; a
/// separate overload of this function takes an animation and a frame. The
/// tree's update functions compute every bone at once and are the costly
/// alternative.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A null output pointer returns false at once. The output is then set to
///   the identity, and an index outside the pivot array returns false with
///   the identity left there.
/// - Walks from the pivot up through its ancestors and composes their base
///   (rest-pose, relative-to-parent) transforms. Each step works on a
///   rotation quaternion and a translation: the accumulated translation is
///   rotated by the ancestor's rotation and added to the ancestor's
///   translation, and the quaternions are multiplied.
/// - The walk stops at the pivot that has no parent, so the root pivot's
///   own base transform is not applied, as in Zero Hour.
/// - Converts the result to a 3x4 matrix and multiplies the object's
///   transform in front of it, which gives the bone's world transform.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version multiplies 3x4 matrices at each step, and it
///       writes the identity to the output before checking that the pointer
///       is not null. BFME 2's quaternion form follows from its pivot
///       layout (see HTreeClass).
///
/// \param pivot_index The bone to evaluate.
/// \param obj_tm      The object's world transform.
/// \param end_tm      [out] Receives the bone's world transform. May be null,
///                    in which case nothing is computed.
/// \return True when the transform was computed; false for a null output or
///         an invalid index.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The quaternion arithmetic is written out inline in the body rather than
/// called, so a change to the pivot transform format (see PivotClass) has to
/// be made here as well as in the tree's update functions.
///
/// \see HTreeClass::Get_Parent_Index, HTreeClass::Init_Default, PivotClass,
///      HTreeClass


/// \class PivotClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief One bone of a hierarchy tree: its name, its parent, its rest-pose
///        and current transforms, its index and its visibility.
///
/// HTreeClass keeps its bones as one array of pivots, and pivot 0 is the
/// root. The rest-pose (base) transform is relative to the parent and comes
/// from the W3D file; the current transform is what the tree's update
/// functions compute each frame; HTreeClass::Simple_Evaluate_Pivot composes
/// base transforms directly.
///
/// \par BFME 2 layout
/// Established from the BFME 2 constructor and copy operations, and
/// described by role on the HTreeClass page:
/// - each transform is a rotation quaternion followed by a translation, not
///   Zero Hour's 3x4 matrix;
/// - a float after the visibility flag starts at 1.0 (the BFME 1
///   reconstruction calls it PivotFade);
/// - Zero Hour's per-pivot capture fields are gone; BFME 2's tree keeps a
///   separate list of captured bones.
///
/// The constructor makes both transforms identity, the parent null, the
/// index 0, the pivot visible, the extra float 1.0 and the name empty.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// A pivot's index is its position in the tree's array. Copying a pivot
/// copies its parent pointer as it is, so the pointer still refers into the
/// source tree; HTreeClass's copy constructor therefore copies each pivot
/// with PivotClass::operator= and then points its parent at the pivot with
/// the same index in the new array.
///
/// \see PivotClass::PivotClass, PivotClass::operator=, HTreeClass,
///      HTreeClass::Get_Parent_Index


/// \fn PivotClass::PivotClass(const PivotClass &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Makes a member-by-member copy of a pivot.
///
/// Copies the parent pointer, both transforms (each rotation, then
/// translation), the index, the visibility flag and the extra float, and
/// copies the 16-byte name last, as Zero Hour's copy constructor does. The
/// parent pointer is copied unchanged; see the class page for how a tree
/// copy fixes it. No direct caller of this constructor is identified.
///
/// \param that The pivot to copy.
///
/// \see PivotClass::operator=, PivotClass


/// \fn PivotClass &PivotClass::operator=(const PivotClass &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Copies another pivot into this one, member by member.
///
/// HTreeClass's copy constructor uses it for each pivot of the new tree,
/// then rebinds the parent pointer by index (see PivotClass). Assigning a
/// pivot to itself does nothing; otherwise the name, the parent pointer,
/// both transforms, the index, the visibility flag and the extra float are
/// copied. Zero Hour's operator also copies the capture state that BFME 2's
/// pivot no longer has.
///
/// \param that The pivot to copy from.
/// \return This pivot.
///
/// \see PivotClass::PivotClass, PivotClass


/// \class LightEnvironmentClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief The lighting of one object, reduced to an ambient colour and at
///        most four lights.
///
/// Zero Hour's header describes the idea: collect the lights that affect an
/// object, keep the strongest, and turn each into a directional light that
/// points from the light towards the object's centre, with distance and
/// spot attenuation folded into its colour; ambient contributions are added
/// into one ambient colour. The usage it gives is to reset the environment
/// with the object's centre and the scene ambient, add the overlapping
/// lights, then call Pre_Render_Update with the camera transform before the
/// object is drawn. DX8Wrapper::Set_Light_Environment passes the result to
/// the device, and Calculate_Fill_Light and Add_Fill_Light can add a
/// computed fill light.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// A plain value type. Each MeshClass holds an environment of its own and
/// a pointer to the environment it renders with. MeshClass::Render passes
/// MeshClass::Set_Lighting_Environment the environment carried in its
/// render information, and Set_Lighting_Environment takes one of two paths
/// depending on that environment's leading byte (see BFME 2 layout below):
/// - Byte set: the mesh stores a pointer to the caller's environment and
///   copies nothing.
/// - Byte clear: the environment is copied into the mesh's own with the
///   assignment operator, and the mesh points at that copy.
/// - A null argument clears the pointer.
///
/// Zero Hour's Set_Lighting_Environment has only the copying path. Other
/// code builds its own environments: the
/// identified callers of the constructor include RTS3DScene's constructor
/// and its per-object rendering (RTS3DScene::renderOneObject,
/// RTS3DScene::Flush), SimpleSceneClass::Customized_Render,
/// W3DProjectedShadowManager's constructor, W3DPropBuffer::drawProps and
/// W3DWaypointBuffer::drawWaypoints.
///
/// \par Hard-coded limits
/// Four input lights and four output lights (Zero Hour's MAX_LIGHTS, which
/// BFME 2's constructor confirms by building four of each). The fill light
/// is kept in a separate field, but when LightEnvironmentClass::Add_Fill_Light
/// adds it, it takes one of the four input slots: the next free one, or the
/// last when all four are used. The light-count limit is not data-driven.
///
/// \par BFME 2 layout
/// The object starts with one extra byte-sized field before the light
/// count, which the constructor clears. Its name and original purpose are
/// unknown. Its observed effect is the selection in
/// MeshClass::Set_Lighting_Environment: a mesh references an environment
/// that has the byte set and copies one that has it clear. The field moves
/// every other member away from where Zero Hour's header puts it, so code
/// built against Zero Hour's lightenvironment.h reads the wrong fields.
///
/// \see LightEnvironmentClass::LightEnvironmentClass, LightClass,
///      \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn LightEnvironmentClass::LightEnvironmentClass(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Creates an empty light environment with every slot cleared.
///
/// Sets the leading BFME 2 field, the light count, the object centre, all
/// four input lights (directions, colours, point-light centres and radii,
/// and both of each light's flags), the output ambient colour, all four
/// output lights, the fill light and the fill intensity to zero or false.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's constructor sets only the light count, the object
///       centre, the output ambient colour, the fill light and the fill
///       intensity, and leaves the per-light slots to be filled when lights
///       are added. BFME 2 clears them all, so an unused slot reads as zero.
///
/// \see LightEnvironmentClass


/// \class FVFInfoClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief A description of a Direct3D flexible vertex format: the format
///        code, the vertex size and where each component sits in a vertex.
///
/// Zero Hour: built from an FVF code (or, for a code of 0, from an explicit
/// vertex size), it records the size of one vertex and the byte offsets of
/// the position, blend weights, normal, diffuse and specular colours and up
/// to eight sets of texture coordinates. Zero Hour's constructor, which is
/// not identified in BFME 2, counts every texture set that is not
/// one-component as two floats, so it mis-sizes three- and four-component
/// sets when it computes the offsets. Vertex buffers keep one so that
/// code filling a buffer can find each component. The formats the engine
/// names are the DX8_FVF_ constants in dx8fvf.h: position only, with
/// normals, with one or two texture coordinate sets, with a diffuse
/// colour, and three special formats: one with three extra three-component
/// coordinate sets (apparently a tangent basis for shaders), one for
/// displacement mapping and one for cube mapping, as the header's revision
/// notes put it.
///
/// \par BFME 2 notes
/// BFME 2's own initialisation of vertex-format descriptors is a larger,
/// four-argument routine that the project reconstructs as a separate
/// structure: next to Zero Hour's fields it records an additional-basis
/// flag (two extra three-float fields), an extension count (a packed byte
/// region and further floats) and the format's index in a table of fifteen
/// known formats. The first thirteen table entries are the DX8_FVF_
/// formats; the last two are pre-transformed formats with a diffuse colour
/// and three or four texture sets. docs/reconstruction/dx8fvf-descriptor.md
/// gives the details. Whether that structure is BFME 2's FVFInfoClass is not
/// established; FVFInfoClass::Get_FVF_Name reads only the format code,
/// which both layouts keep first.
///
/// \see FVFInfoClass::Get_FVF_Name, Get_FVF_Vertex_Size


/// \fn void FVFInfoClass::Get_FVF_Name(StringClass &fvfname) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_ww3d_renderobj_htree_helpers
/// \brief Writes a readable name for the vertex format, such as
///        "D3DFVF_XYZ|D3DFVF_NORMAL|D3DFVF_TEX1", into a string.
///
/// A debugging aid (Zero Hour's header marks it so). No direct caller is
/// identified in BFME 2.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A switch on the format code with one case for each of the thirteen
/// DX8_FVF_ formats; any other code gives "Unknown!". The unit compiles
/// against Zero Hour's dx8fvf.h, so the case values are Zero Hour's
/// constants. The text for the cube-map format names a texture set and a
/// three-component texture coordinate that the cube-map constant itself
/// does not include (Zero Hour's header has that part commented out), and
/// the string misspells one flag and leaves a parenthesis unclosed; the
/// retail executable carries the same text.
///
/// \param fvfname [out] Receives the name.
///
/// \see FVFInfoClass, Get_FVF_Vertex_Size


/// \fn unsigned Get_FVF_Vertex_Size(unsigned FVF)
/// \brief Returns the size in bytes of one vertex of a flexible vertex
///        format, as D3DX computes it.
///
/// A file-local wrapper around D3DXGetFVFVertexSize. Zero Hour:
/// FVFInfoClass's constructor uses it for the vertex size when the format
/// code is not 0; that constructor is not identified in BFME 2. BFME 2's
/// larger descriptor routine (see FVFInfoClass) calls the D3DX function
/// directly, and no caller of this wrapper is identified.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The retail body is three instructions: it pushes the format code, which
/// arrives in a register, calls the imported D3DX function and returns.
/// The import is D3DXGetFVFVertexSize from d3dx9_27.dll, the D3DX 9
/// runtime, although the source declares it from the DirectX 8 headers.
///
/// \param FVF A Direct3D flexible-vertex-format code.
/// \return The vertex size in bytes.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is one of the places the executable depends on the D3DX 9
/// redistributable. A replacement must reproduce D3DX's FVF size rules as
/// Direct3D's FVF documentation gives them. The components counted include
/// the position or pre-transformed position (two of BFME 2's known formats
/// use the latter), blend weights, normal, point size (D3DFVF_PSIZE, one
/// float), diffuse and specular colours, and each texture set's size code
/// up to the format's texture count. docs/reconstruction/dx8fvf-descriptor.md
/// describes how BFME 2's descriptor routine in this unit adds its own
/// extra fields to the D3DX size and lays out the component offsets.
///
/// \see FVFInfoClass, FVFInfoClass::Get_FVF_Name
